 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 233491,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "There are years of drought in a small, relatively isolated community. During the drought, small seeds with thin shells become rare. Large seeds with hard cases become increasingly common. The large, tough seeds are successfully eaten by birds with large and broad beaks. Assuming that the drought continues and the population of birds in the community stays isolated, what predictions for the population can you make under the influence of natural selection?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 724107,
     "content_html": "The birds with small, thin beaks will grow larger, broader beaks to be able to eat the larger seeds. This will result in subsequent generations having a higher percentage of birds with large, broad beaks."
    },
    {
     "id": 724110,
     "content_html": "There will be more birds with small, thin beaks dying and more birds with large, broad beaks surviving. Differential reproduction of birds with large, broad beaks will result in subsequent generations having a higher percentage of birds with large, broad beaks."
    },
    {
     "id": 724108,
     "content_html": "The species will diverge into two species, one with small, thin beaks and one with large, broad beaks. The two species will then compete for resources."
    },
    {
     "id": 724109,
     "content_html": "There will be neither phenotypic nor genotypic changes in the population."
    }
   ],
